Graph Traversal

The process of visiting all vertices.

Two common traversal algorithms are:

Depth-First Search (DFS): Explores as far as possible along one path before backtracking. Uses a stack (or recursion).
Breadth-First Search (BFS): Visits all neighboring vertices level by level. Uses a queue.

Breadth-First Search (BFS)

Definition:
BFS is a graph traversal algorithm that visits vertices level by level. It first visits all neighbors of the current node before moving to the next level.

Data Structure Used: Queue (FIFO)

BFS Algorithm
Start from the source node.
Mark it as visited.
Add it to the queue.
Remove the front node from the queue.
Visit all its unvisited neighbors and add them to the queue.
Repeat until the queue is empty.

